<edge source="Person:wassily kandinsky" target="Institution:bauhaus" id="TAUGHT_AT|1149">
  <data key="d3">TAUGHT_AT</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Wassily_Kandinsky.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Kandinsky taught the basic design class for beginners and the course on advanced theory at the Bauhaus; he also conducted painting classes and a workshop in which he augmented his colour theory with new elements of form psychology.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:wassily kandinsky" target="Institution:bauhaus" id="FOUNDED|1153">
  <data key="d3">FOUNDED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Weimar.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Later, artists and architects including Henry van de Velde, Wassily Kandinsky, Paul Klee, Lyonel Feininger, and Walter Gropius came to the city and founded the Bauhaus movement, the most important German design school of the interwar period.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:wassily kandinsky" target="Institution:academy of fine arts" id="STUDIED_AT|1138">
  <data key="d3">STUDIED_AT</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Wassily_Kandinsky.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1896, Kandinsky settled in Munich, studying first at Anton Ažbe's private school and then at the Academy of Fine Arts.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:wassily kandinsky" target="Work:sunday, old russia" id="DESIGNED|1140">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Wassily_Kandinsky.md</data>
  <data key="d5">For the most part, however, Kandinsky's paintings did not feature any human figures; an exception is Sunday, Old Russia (1904), in which Kandinsky recreates a highly colourful (and fanciful) view of peasants and nobles in front of the walls of a town.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:wassily kandinsky" target="Work:couple on horseback" id="DESIGNED|1141">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Wassily_Kandinsky.md</data>
