  <data key="d5">The most famous designer of the Art Nouveau period, René Lalique, served his apprenticeship in the Aucoc studio from 1874 to 1876.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:louis cartier" target="Work:products" id="DESIGNED|57">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Deco.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Prominent businesses such as silverware firm Christofle, glass designer René Lalique, and the jewellers Louis Cartier and Boucheron began designing products in more modern styles.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:boucheron" target="Work:products" id="DESIGNED|58">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Deco.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Prominent businesses such as silverware firm Christofle, glass designer René Lalique, and the jewellers Louis Cartier and Boucheron began designing products in more modern styles.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:paul follot" target="Work:gobelin carpet" id="DESIGNED|59">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Deco.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Many colourful works, including chairs and a table by Maurice Dufrêne and a bright Gobelin carpet by Paul Follot were presented at the 1912 Salon des artistes décorateurs.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:adrien karbowsky" target="Work:floral chair" id="DESIGNED|60">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Deco.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1912–1913 designer Adrien Karbowsky made a floral chair with a parrot design for the hunting lodge of art collector Jacques Doucet.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Institution:perret's office" id="STUDIED_AT|62">
  <data key="d3">STUDIED_AT</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Deco.md</data>
  <data key="d5">From 1908 to 1910, the 21-year-old Le Corbusier worked as a draftsman in Perret's office, learning the techniques of concrete construction.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:pavilion de l'esprit nouveau" id="DESIGNED|69">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Deco.md</data>
  <data key="d5">While most of the pavilions were lavishly decorated and filled with hand-made luxury furniture, two pavilions, those of the Soviet Union and Pavilion de L'Esprit Nouveau, built by the magazine of that name run by Le Corbusier, were built in an austere style with plain white walls and no decoration; they were among the earliest examples of modernist architecture.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Movement:the french union of modern artists" id="BELONGS_TO|73">
  <data key="d3">BELONGS_TO</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Deco.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Its members included architects Pierre Chareau, Francis Jourdain, Robert Mallet-Stevens, Le Corbusier, and, in the Soviet Union, Konstantin Melnikov; the Irish designer Eileen Gray; the French designer Sonia Delaunay; and the jewellers Georges Fouquet and Jean Puiforcat.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Movement:the french union of modern artists" id="BELONGS_TO|1254">
  <data key="d3">BELONGS_TO</data>
  <data key="d4">ko_아르데코.md</data>
  <data key="d5">회원으로는 건축가 피에르 샤로, 프랑시스 주르댕, 로베르 말레 스테방스, 르 코르뷔지에, 그리고 소련의 콘스탄틴 멜니코프, 아일랜드 디자이너 아일린 그레이, 프랑스 디자이너 소니아 들로네, 보석 세공인 조르주 푸케와 장 퓌포르카 등이 있었다.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villa fallet" id="DESIGNED|213">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Nouveau.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Another notable building in the style is the Villa Fallet La Chaux-de-Fonds, a chalet designed and built in 1905 by a student of L'Eplattenier, the eighteen-year-old Le Corbusier.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villa fallet" id="DESIGNED|609">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1905, he and two other students, under the supervision of their teacher, René Chapallaz, designed and built his first house, the Villa Fallet, for the engraver Louis Fallet, a friend of his teacher Charles L'Eplattenier.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:lc4 chaise longue" id="DESIGNED|607">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Le Corbusier also designed well-known furniture such as the LC4 chaise longue and the LC1 chair, both made of leather with metal framing.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:lc1 chair" id="DESIGNED|608">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Le Corbusier also designed well-known furniture such as the LC4 chaise longue and the LC1 chair, both made of leather with metal framing.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villas jacquemet" id="DESIGNED|610">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The success of this house led to his construction of two similar houses, the Villas Jacquemet and Stotzer, in the same area.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villas stotzer" id="DESIGNED|611">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The success of this house led to his construction of two similar houses, the Villas Jacquemet and Stotzer, in the same area.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:jeanneret-perret house" id="DESIGNED|612">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1912, he began his most ambitious project yet: a new house for his parents, also located on the forested hillside near La-Chaux-de-Fonds.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Person:rené chapallaz" id="INFLUENCED_BY|614">
  <data key="d3">INFLUENCED_BY</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">His architecture teacher was architect René Chapallaz, who had a large influence on Le Corbusier's earliest house designs.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:dom-ino house" id="DESIGNED|615">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">This led him to his plan for the Dom-Ino House (1914–15).</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:schwob house" id="DESIGNED|616">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In August 1916, Le Corbusier received his largest commission ever, to construct a villa for the Swiss watchmaker Anatole Schwob, for whom he had already completed several small remodelling projects.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Person:auguste perret" id="INFLUENCED_BY|617">
  <data key="d3">INFLUENCED_BY</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">You can see, he wrote to Auguste Perret in July 1916, that Auguste Perret left more in me than Peter Behrens.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Person:peter behrens" id="INFLUENCED_BY|618">
  <data key="d3">INFLUENCED_BY</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">You can see, he wrote to Auguste Perret in July 1916, that Auguste Perret left more in me than Peter Behrens.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Movement:purism" id="BELONGS_TO|619">
  <data key="d3">BELONGS_TO</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Rejecting Cubism as irrational and "romantic", the pair jointly published their manifesto, Après le Cubisme and established a new artistic movement, Purism.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Movement:purism" id="BELONGS_TO|626">
  <data key="d3">BELONGS_TO</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Le Corbusier and Ozenfant had broken with Cubism and formed the Purism movement in 1918 and in 1920 founded their journal L'Esprit Nouveau.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Movement:purism" id="BELONGS_TO|1166">
  <data key="d3">BELONGS_TO</data>
  <data key="d4">ko_기능주의_(건축).md</data>
  <data key="d5">한편 “집은 살기 위한 기계”라는 유명한 말을 남긴 프랑스의 건축가 르 코르뷔지에Le Corbusier(1887~1965)는 1924년경부터 회화 운동의 하나인 순수주의의 원리를 건축에 응용하였다.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Institution:union of modern artists" id="FOUNDED|621">
  <data key="d3">FOUNDED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">They resigned and founded the Union of Modern Artists ("Union des artistes modernes": UAM).</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:maison citrohan" id="DESIGNED|624">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Among these, was the Maison "Citrohan."</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:esprit nouveau pavilion" id="DESIGNED|625">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">An important early work of Le Corbusier was the Esprit Nouveau Pavilion, built for the 1925 Paris International Exhibition of Modern Decorative and Industrial Arts, the event which later gave Art Deco its name.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:esprit nouveau pavilion" id="DESIGNED|872">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Only two pavilions were purely modernist; the Esprit Nouveau pavilion of Le Corbusier, which represented his idea for a mass-produced housing unit, and the pavilion of the USSR, by Konstantin Melnikov in a flamboyantly futurist style.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:plan voisin" id="DESIGNED|628">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Besides the furniture, the pavilion exhibited a model of his Plan Voisin, his provocative plan for rebuilding a large part of the centre of Paris.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:l'art décoratif d'aujourd'hui" id="DESIGNED|629">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1925, Le Corbusier combined a series of articles about decorative art from L'Esprit Nouveau into a book, L'art décoratif d'aujourd'hui (The Decorative Art of Today).</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:maison la roche/albert jeanneret" id="DESIGNED|630">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">These included the Maison La Roche/Albert Jeanneret (1923–1925), which now houses the Fondation Le Corbusier; the Maison Guiette in Antwerp, Belgium (1926); a residence for Jacques Lipchitz; the Maison Cook; and the Maison Planeix.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villa savoy" id="DESIGNED|631">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The following year he began the Villa Savoye (1928–1931), which became one of the most famous of Le Corbusier's works and an icon of modernist architecture.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villa savoy" id="DESIGNED|640">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Like the Villa Savoye, the structure was poised on concrete pylons though, because of the shortage of steel to reinforce the concrete, the pylons were more massive than usual.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villa savoy" id="DESIGNED|651">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1959 a successful international campaign was launched to have his Villa Savoye, threatened with demolition, declared a historic monument; it was the first time that a work by a living architect had received this distinction.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villa savoy" id="DESIGNED|657">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The "Architectural Promenade" was another idea dear to Le Corbusier, which he particularly put into play in his design of the Villa Savoye.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villa savoy" id="DESIGNED|673">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1928: Villa Savoye, Poissy-sur-Seine, France</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villa savoy" id="DESIGNED|852">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The best-known of these houses was the Villa Savoye, built in 1928–1931 in the Paris suburb of Poissy.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villa savoy" id="DESIGNED|1191">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">ko_르_코르뷔지에.md</data>
  <data key="d5">르 코르뷔지에는 빌라 사부아(Villa Savoye, 1929-1931)에서 그가 잡지 "레스프리 누보"와 그의 저서 "건축을 향하여"를 통해 설명한, 1920년대에 걸쳐 확립한 개념인 건축의 다섯 가지 요소를 분명하게 종합한다.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:cité frugès" id="DESIGNED|632">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1926, Le Corbusier received the opportunity he had been looking for; he was commissioned by a Bordeaux industrialist, Henry Frugès, a fervent admirer of his ideas on urban planning, to build a complex of worker housing, the Cité Frugès, at Pessac, a suburb of Bordeaux.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:cité frugès" id="DESIGNED|672">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1926: Cité Frugès, Pessac, France</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:tsentrosoyuz" id="DESIGNED|633">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1928 he received a commission from the Soviet government to construct the headquarters of the Tsentrosoyuz, or central office of trade unions, a large office building whose glass walls alternated with plaques of stone.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:tsentrosoyuz" id="DESIGNED|678">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1933: Tsentrosoyuz, Moscow, USSR</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:villa de madrot" id="DESIGNED|634">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">He built the Villa de Madrot in Le Pradet (1929–1931); and an apartment in Paris for Charles de Bestigui at the top of an existing building on the Champs-Élysées (1929–1932), (later demolished).</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:cité de refuge" id="DESIGNED|635">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Between 1929 and 1933, he built a larger and more ambitious project for the Salvation Army, the Cité de Refuge, on rue Cantagrel in the 13th arrondissement of Paris.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:swiss pavilion" id="DESIGNED|636">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">He also constructed the Swiss Pavilion in the Cité Universitaire in Paris with 46 units of student housing, (1929–33).</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:immeuble clarté" id="DESIGNED|637">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In Geneva, he built a glass-walled apartment building with 45 units, the Immeuble Clarté.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:immeuble clarté" id="DESIGNED|677">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1931: Immeuble Clarté, Geneva, Switzerland</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:maison loucheur" id="DESIGNED|638">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Le Corbusier immediately began to design a new type of modular housing unit, which he called the Maison Loucheur, which would be suitable for the project.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:unité d'habitation" id="DESIGNED|639">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Dautry agreed to fund one of his projects, a "Unité habitation de grandeur conforme", or housing units of standard size, with the first one to be built in Marseille, which had been heavily damaged during the war.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:unité d'habitation" id="DESIGNED|645">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">For the living space he used the same Modulor concept for measuring the ideal living space that he had used in the Unité d'Habitation in Marseille; height under the ceiling of 2.26 metres (7 feet 5 inches); and width 1.83 metres (6 feet 0 inches).</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:unité d'habitation" id="DESIGNED|679">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1947–1952: Unité d'Habitation, Marseille, France</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:unité d'habitation" id="DESIGNED|892">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">He called it Unité d'Habitation in Marseille, but it more popularly took the name of the Cité Radieuse (and later "Cité du Fada" "City of the crazy one" in Marseille French), after his book about futuristic urban planning.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:unité d'habitation" id="DESIGNED|1194">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">ko_르_코르뷔지에.md</data>
  <data key="d5">그가 설계한 유니테 다비타시옹(United d'Habitation) 또한 모뒬로르 체계가 적용된 건물중 하나이다.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:cité radieuse" id="DESIGNED|641">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">He gave the building the name of his pre-war theoretical project, the Cité Radieuse, and followed the principles that he had studied before the war, proposing a giant reinforced-concrete framework into which modular apartments would fit like bottles into a bottle rack.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:cité radieuse" id="DESIGNED|666">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">This mission statement manifested itself in the form of "Cité Radieuse" (The Radiant City), located in Marseille, France.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:factory for the company of claude and duval" id="DESIGNED|642">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Le Corbusier made another almost identical Unité d'Habitation in Rezé-les-Nantes in the Loire-Atlantique Department between 1948 and 1952, and three more over the following years, in Berlin, Briey-en-Forêt and Firminy; and he designed a factory for the company of Claude and Duval, in Saint-Dié in the Vosges.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:notre-dame-du-haut" id="DESIGNED|643">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In the postwar years, he designed two important religious buildings; the chapel of Notre-Dame-du-Haut at Ronchamp (1950–1955); and the Convent of Sainte Marie de La Tourette (1953–1960).</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:convent of sainte marie de la tourette" id="DESIGNED|644">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In the postwar years, he designed two important religious buildings; the chapel of Notre-Dame-du-Haut at Ronchamp (1950–1955); and the Convent of Sainte Marie de La Tourette (1953–1960).</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:convent of sainte marie de la tourette" id="DESIGNED|936">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">His most prominent late work was the convent of Sainte Marie de La Tourette in Eveux-sur-l'Arbresle.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:church of saint pierre" id="DESIGNED|646">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1960, Le Corbusier began a third religious building, the Church of Saint Pierre in the new town of Firminy-Vert, where he had built a Unité d'Habitation and a cultural and sports centre.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:chandigarh" id="DESIGNED|647">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Le Corbusier's largest and most ambitious project was the design of Chandigarh, the capital city of the Punjab and Haryana States of India, created after India received independence in 1947.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:high court of justice" id="DESIGNED|648">
