<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:lc4" id="DESIGNED|660">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Le_Corbusier.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The first results of the collaboration between Le Corbusier and Perriand were three types of chairs made with chrome-plated tubular steel frames: The LC4, Chaise Longue, (1927–28), with a covering of cowhide, which gave it a touch of exoticism; the Fauteuil Grand Confort (LC3) (1928–29), a club chair with a tubular frame which resembled the comfortable Art Deco club chairs that became popular in the 1920s; and the Fauteuil à dossier vascular (LC4) (1928–29), a low seat suspended in a tubular steel frame, also with cowhide upholstery.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:le corbusier" target="Work:fauteuil grand confort (lc3)" id="DESIGNED|661">
