<edge source="Person:konstantin melnikov" target="Work:rusakov workers' club" id="DESIGNED|867">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">One of the first prominent constructivist architects to emerge in Moscow was Konstantin Melnikov, the number of working clubs – including Rusakov Workers' Club (1928) – and his own living house, Melnikov House (1929) near Arbat Street in Moscow.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:konstantin melnikov" target="Work:melnikov house" id="DESIGNED|868">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">One of the first prominent constructivist architects to emerge in Moscow was Konstantin Melnikov, the number of working clubs – including Rusakov Workers' Club (1928) – and his own living house, Melnikov House (1929) near Arbat Street in Moscow.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:konstantin melnikov" target="Work:soviet pavilion" id="DESIGNED|869">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Melnikov traveled to Paris in 1925 where he built the Soviet Pavilion for the International Exhibition of Modern Decorative and Industrial Arts in Paris in 1925; it was a highly geometric vertical construction of glass and steel crossed by a diagonal stairway, and crowned with a hammer and sickle.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:konstantin melnikov" target="Work:pavilion of the ussr" id="DESIGNED|873">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Only two pavilions were purely modernist; the Esprit Nouveau pavilion of Le Corbusier, which represented his idea for a mass-produced housing unit, and the pavilion of the USSR, by Konstantin Melnikov in a flamboyantly futurist style.</data>
</edge>
