<edge source="Person:albert speer" target="Work:pavilion of nazi germany" id="DESIGNED|80">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Deco.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The last buildings built in Paris in the new style were the Museum of Public Works by Auguste Perret (now the French Economic, Social and Environmental Council), the Palais de Chaillot by Louis-Hippolyte Boileau, Jacques Carlu and Léon Azéma, and the Palais de Tokyo of the 1937 Paris International Exposition; they looked out at the grandiose pavilion of Nazi Germany, designed by Albert Speer, which faced the equally grandiose socialist-realist pavilion of Stalin's Soviet Union.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:albert speer" target="Work:pavilions of nazi germany" id="DESIGNED|882">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The pavilions of Nazi Germany, designed by Albert Speer, in a German neoclassical style topped by eagle and swastika, faced the pavilion of the Soviet Union, topped by enormous statues of a worker and a peasant carrying a hammer and sickle.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:tamara de lempicka" target="Person:maurice denis" id="INFLUENCED_BY|81">
  <data key="d3">INFLUENCED_BY</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Deco.md</data>
  <data key="d5">She studied under Maurice Denis and André Lhote, and borrowed many elements from their styles.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:tamara de lempicka" target="Person:andré lhote" id="INFLUENCED_BY|82">
