  <data key="d5">In 1893, Hankar designed and built the Hankar House, his own residence in Brussels.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:paul hankar" target="Work:hankar house" id="DESIGNED|245">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Nouveau.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The style first appeared in Brussels' Hankar House by Paul Hankar (1893) and Hôtel Tassel (1892–93) of Victor Horta.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:paul hankar" target="Institution:royal academy of fine arts" id="STUDIED_AT|124">
  <data key="d3">STUDIED_AT</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Nouveau.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Born at Frameries, in Hainaut, the son of a master stone cutter, he had studied ornamental sculpture and decoration at the Royal Academy of Fine Arts in Brussels from 1873 to 1884, whilst working as an ornamental sculptor.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:paul hankar" target="Person:viollet-le-duc" id="INFLUENCED_BY|133">
  <data key="d3">INFLUENCED_BY</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Nouveau.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Hankar was influenced by both Viollet-le-Duc and the ideas of the English Arts and Crafts movement.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Work:houses" id="DESIGNED|113">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Nouveau.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The first Art Nouveau houses and interior decoration appeared in Brussels in the 1890s, in the architecture and interior design of houses designed by Paul Hankar, Henry van de Velde, and especially Victor Horta, whose Hôtel Tassel was completed in 1893.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Work:villa bloemenwerf" id="DESIGNED|126">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Nouveau.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Van de Velde's designs included the interior of his residence in Brussels, the Villa Bloemenwerf (1895).</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Institution:grand-ducal school of arts and crafts" id="FOUNDED|127">
  <data key="d3">FOUNDED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Nouveau.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1906, he departed Belgium for Weimar (Germany), where he founded the Grand-Ducal School of Arts and Crafts, where the teaching of historical styles was forbidden.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Work:maison de l'art nouveau" id="DESIGNED|128">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Nouveau.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The interior and furniture of the gallery were designed by the Belgian architect Henry van de Velde, one of the pioneers of Art Nouveau architecture.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Movement:art nouveau" id="INFLUENCED_BY|134">
  <data key="d3">INFLUENCED_BY</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Nouveau.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Other important Art Nouveau artists from Belgium included the architect and designer Henry van de Velde, though the most important part of his career was spent in Germany; he strongly influenced the decoration of the Jugendstil.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Movement:art nouveau" id="BELONGS_TO|256">
  <data key="d3">BELONGS_TO</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Nouveau.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In Belgium, the pioneer architects of the Art Nouveau movement, Victor Horta and Henry van de Velde, designed furniture for their houses, using vigorous curving lines and a minimum of decoration.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Work:la veillée d'anges" id="DESIGNED|264">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Art_Nouveau.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The Belgian Henry van de Velde presented a textile work, La Veillée d'Anges, at the Salon La Libre Esthéthique in Brussels, inspired by the symbolism of Paul Gauguin and of the Nabis.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Institution:grand ducal saxon school of arts and crafts" id="FOUNDED|281">
  <data key="d3">FOUNDED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Bauhaus.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Its roots lay in the arts and crafts school founded by the Grand Duke of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach in 1906, and directed by Belgian Art Nouveau architect Henry van de Velde.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Work:theater" id="DESIGNED|371">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Deutscher_Werkbund.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The Werkbund commissioned van de Velde to design a theater for the 1914 Werkbund Exhibition in Cologne.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Institution:bauhaus" id="FOUNDED|1152">
  <data key="d3">FOUNDED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Weimar.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Later, artists and architects including Henry van de Velde, Wassily Kandinsky, Paul Klee, Lyonel Feininger, and Walter Gropius came to the city and founded the Bauhaus movement, the most important German design school of the interwar period.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Institution:kunstgewerbeschule weimar" id="FOUNDED|1157">
  <data key="d3">FOUNDED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Weimar.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The Kunstgewerbeschule Weimar was found by Henry van de Velde with the support of Grand Duke William Ernest in 1902 and represents the other root of Bauhaus, known as "Das Neue Weimar" ("The New Weimar") around Harry Graf Kessler.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:henry van de velde" target="Work:bauhaus university" id="DESIGNED|1161">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Weimar.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Both were built by Henry van de Velde between 1904 and 1911.</data>
</edge>
