  <data key="d3">BELONGS_TO</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Constructivism_(art).md</data>
  <data key="d5">Constructivism (Russian: конструктивизм, romanized: konstruktivizm) is an early twentieth-century art movement founded in 1915 by Vladimir Tatlin and Alexander Rodchenko.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:vladimir tatlin" target="Work:monument to the third international" id="DESIGNED|338">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Constructivism_(art).md</data>
  <data key="d5">The key work of Constructivism was Vladimir Tatlin's proposal for the Monument to the Third International (1919–20), which is known as "Tatlin's Tower".</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movement:constructivism" target="Movement:bauhaus" id="INFLUENCED_BY|336">
  <data key="d3">INFLUENCED_BY</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Constructivism_(art).md</data>
  <data key="d5">Constructivist architecture and art had a great effect on modern art movements of the 20th century, influencing major trends such as the Bauhaus and De Stijl movements.</data>
</edge>
