<edge source="Person:philip johnson" target="Work:munson-williams-proctor arts institute" id="DESIGNED|1077">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Philip_Johnson.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The Munson-Williams-Proctor Arts Institute (1960) is a good example of his work in the period; it is supported by eight external ferro-concrete piers, or two on each side.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:philip johnson" target="Work:pennzoil place" id="DESIGNED|1078">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Philip_Johnson.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The most prominent of these was Pennzoil Place (1970–76) in Houston, Texas.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:philip johnson" target="Work:johnson building of the boston central library" id="DESIGNED|1079">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Philip_Johnson.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The Johnson building of the Boston Central Library (1972) adjoins the original Boston library built in the 19th century by the celebrated firm of McKim, Mead &amp; White.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:philip johnson" target="Work:fort worth water gardens" id="DESIGNED|1080">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Philip_Johnson.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The Fort Worth Water Gardens, which opened in 1974, is an urban landscape where visitors experience water in distinct ways.</data>
</edge>
