</edge>
<edge source="Person:donna karan" target="Work:capsule workwear pieces" id="DESIGNED|830">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Minimalism.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The concept was further popularized in the next decade by American fashion designer Donna Karan, who designed a seminal collection of capsule workwear pieces in 1985.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:joseph paxton" target="Work:the crystal palace" id="DESIGNED|832">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The Crystal Palace by Joseph Paxton at the Great Exhibition of 1851 was an early example of iron and plate glass construction, followed in 1864 by the first glass and metal curtain wall.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:william le baron jenney" target="Work:home insurance building" id="DESIGNED|833">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The developments together led to the first steel-framed skyscraper, the ten-story Home Insurance Building in Chicago, built in 1884 by William Le Baron Jenney and based on the works of Viollet le Duc.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:william le baron jenney" target="Work:the home insurance building" id="DESIGNED|849">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">It was designed by William Le Baron Jenney in 1883, and was briefly the tallest building in the world.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:antonio gaudi" target="Work:casa batlló" id="DESIGNED|835">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In Barcelona, Antoni Gaudi conceived architecture as a form of sculpture; the façade of the Casa Batlló in Barcelona (1904–1906) had no straight lines; it was encrusted with colorful mosaics of stone and ceramic tiles.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:cass gilbert" target="Work:woolworth building" id="DESIGNED|851">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The Woolworth Building, designed by Cass Gilbert, was completed in 1912, and was the tallest building in the world until the completion of the Chrysler Building in 1929.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:hermann finsterlin" target="Work:formspiels" id="DESIGNED|859">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">As result, many of the most innovative expressionist projects, including Bruno Taut's Alpine Architecture and Hermann Finsterlin's Formspiels, remained on paper.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:erich mendelsohn" target="Work:einsteinium" id="DESIGNED|860">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1920, he finally was able to construct one of his works in the city of Potsdam; an observatory and research center called the Einsteinium, named in tribute to Albert Einstein.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:erich mendelsohn" target="Work:mossehaus" id="DESIGNED|861">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">His Mossehaus in Berlin was an early model for the streamline moderne style.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:erich mendelsohn" target="Work:columbushaus" id="DESIGNED|862">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">His Columbushaus on Potsdamer Platz in Berlin (1931) was a prototype for the modernist office buildings that followed.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:erich mendelsohn" target="Work:모세 하우스" id="DESIGNED|1280">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">ko_아르데코.md</data>
  <data key="d5">에리히 멘델손의 모세 하우스와 베를린의 샤우뷔네, 함부르크의 칠레하우스 등이 주목할 만한 사례이다.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:fritz höger" target="Work:chilehaus" id="DESIGNED|863">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">His Chilehaus was built as the headquarters of a shipping company, and was modeled after a giant steamship, a triangular building with a sharply pointed bow.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:hans poelzig" target="Work:großes schauspielhaus" id="DESIGNED|864">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">In 1919 he built the Großes Schauspielhaus, an immense theater in Berlin, seating five thousand spectators for theater impresario Max Reinhardt.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:hans poelzig" target="Work:ig farben building" id="DESIGNED|865">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">He also constructed the IG Farben building, a massive corporate headquarters, now the main building of Goethe University in Frankfurt.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:rudolf steiner" target="Work:second goetheanum" id="DESIGNED|866">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">His Second Goetheanum, built from 1926 near Basel, Switzerland and Mendelsohn's Einsteinturm in Potsdam, Germany, were based on no traditional models and had entirely original shapes.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:moisei ginzburg" target="Work:narkomfin building" id="DESIGNED|870">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">This group created several major constructivist projects in the wake of the First Five Year Plan – including colossal Dnieper Hydroelectric Station (1932) – and made an attempt to start the standardization of living blocks with Ginzburg's Narkomfin building.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:rudolph schindler" target="Work:schindler house" id="DESIGNED|878">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The Austrian architect Rudolph Schindler designed what could be called the first house in the modern style in 1922, the Schindler house.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:rudolph schindler" target="Work:lovell beach house" id="DESIGNED|879">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Schindler also contributed to American modernism with his design for the Lovell Beach House in Newport Beach.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:richard neutra" target="Work:lovell health house" id="DESIGNED|880">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The Austrian architect Richard Neutra moved to the United States in 1923, worked for a short time with Frank Lloyd Wright, also quickly became a force in American architecture through his modernist design for the same client, the Lovell Health House in Los Angeles.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:richard neutra" target="Work:kaufmann desert house" id="DESIGNED|881">
  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modern_architecture.md</data>
  <data key="d5">Neutra's most notable architectural work was the Kaufmann Desert House in 1946, and he designed hundreds of further projects.</data>
</edge>
