  <data key="d3">DESIGNED</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modernism.md</data>
  <data key="d5">American Gothic is a painting by Grant Wood from 1930 portraying a pitchfork-holding farmer and a younger woman in front of a house of Carpenter Gothic style, it is one of the most familiar images in 20th-century American art.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:hans hofmann" target="Institution:america" id="TAUGHT_AT|993">
  <data key="d3">TAUGHT_AT</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modernism.md</data>
  <data key="d5">The 1940s in New York City heralded the triumph of American Abstract Expressionism, a modernist movement that combined lessons learned from Henri Matisse, Pablo Picasso, Surrealism, Joan Miró, Cubism, Fauvism, and early modernism via great teachers in America like Hans Hofmann and John D. Graham.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person:john d. graham" target="Institution:america" id="TAUGHT_AT|994">
  <data key="d3">TAUGHT_AT</data>
  <data key="d4">en_Modernism.md</data>
